"How Long Can a Star Shine? | VirtualDemos",
"How Long Can a Star Shine?",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} to §{#} (the Sun’s energy, mass and energy, the solar interior) and §{#} (main sequence lifetimes). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> four modes, chosen above the picture. Thermostat: Squeeze the core, Puff it up, Fusion runs fast, Fusion runs slow, Lid on: a runaway. Fuel: The Sun, {#} M☉ at {#} L☉, Sirius A, A red dwarf. Light’s long walk: Leaving a concert, The Sun with {#} mm steps, The real core. Neutrinos: The race out, The solar neutrino problem, If neutrinos never changed. This worksheet uses <strong>Fuel</strong> and <strong>Thermostat</strong>.",
"Fuel weighs four hydrogen atoms against one helium atom and turns the missing mass into energy with E = mc². The <strong>Star mass</strong> and <strong>Luminosity</strong> sliders set a star; click either readout above its slider to type an exact value. The readouts give the star’s lifetime, the mass it turns into energy each second and the Sun’s lifetime for comparison.",
"Thermostat shows the Sun cut open. Each setup starts the core a little out of balance; <strong>Play</strong> runs it forward at {#} million years each second, and the graph shows the core’s temperature and the fusion power. It stops by itself after {#} million years.",
"Record reading (Fuel mode only) stores the setup’s name, the mass, the luminosity and the lifetime. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the first data table. Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"The model: a star fuses the {#} % of its mass in its core, and {#} % of that mass becomes energy. The thermostat treats the whole Sun as a ball of ideal gas that keeps its shape as it grows or shrinks, with the light leaving held at today’s value.",
"Work out how long the Sun and other stars can shine on their hydrogen, find why the heaviest stars die first, and see what keeps the Sun’s fusion from running away.",
"<strong>Mass into energy.</strong> Deep in the Sun’s core, at about {#} million K, hydrogen nuclei fuse: four of them end up as one helium nucleus. A helium atom has {#} % less mass than the four hydrogen atoms that made it. That missing mass has become energy, as much as Einstein’s",
"\\[E = mc^2\\]",
"gives, where \\(c = 3.00\\times10^8\\) m/s. Because \\(c^2\\) is so large, a little mass is a lot of energy: one gram is \\(9\\times10^{13}\\) J, about what a large power plant makes in a day.",
"<strong>Fuel and burn rate.</strong> A star shines with its luminosity \\(L\\), the energy it gives off each second; the Sun’s is \\(L_\\odot = 3.83\\times10^{26}\\) W. Only the core is hot enough to fuse, so a star like the Sun uses about {#} % of its mass \\(M\\) before it runs out of hydrogen there. The energy it has to spend is then \\(0.10 \\times 0.0071 \\times Mc^2\\), and spending it at the rate \\(L\\) takes",
"\\[t = \\frac{0.10 \\times 0.0071 \\times M c^2}{L}.\\]",
"The lifetime is the fuel divided by the burn rate, like a car’s range is its fuel divided by how fast it uses it. In units of the Sun’s mass (\\(M_\\odot = 1.99\\times10^{30}\\) kg) and luminosity, this becomes \\(t \\approx 10\\) billion years \\(\\times (M/M_\\odot)/(L/L_\\odot)\\): a star’s lifetime goes as the ratio \\(M/L\\).",
"<strong>Why a star stays steady.</strong> The Sun’s own gravity pulls all its gas inward, and the pressure of the hot gas pushes out. In the core the two balance, and fusion makes just as much energy each second as the surface sends off as light. The Sun has done this for {#} billion years with very little change. What happens if the balance is upset is the subject of the last part of this lab.",
"A kilogram of hydrogen is fused into helium, and {#} % of its mass disappears. How much mass is that, and how much energy does it become? A {#} W light bulb uses {#} J each second; how long could this energy run one?",
"Use the lifetime formula in the Background to find the Sun’s lifetime in seconds, then in years (one year is \\(3.16\\times10^7\\) s).",
"A star has {#} times the Sun’s mass and {#} times its luminosity. Without the formula: how many times the fuel does it have, how many times faster does it burn it, and what is its lifetime compared with the Sun’s?",
"Imagine the nuclear reactions in the Sun’s core suddenly ran {#} times faster than they do now, at the same temperature. The core now makes more energy than the surface lets out. Once the Sun has settled down again, will its core be hotter than today, cooler than today, or the same? Write your guess and the reason for it before you open the demo.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Fuel.</strong> Open the demo and choose <strong>Fuel</strong> above the picture.",
